WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.788 --> 00:00:11.269
Aisha Coleman: Like, Morning, both. How's Dallas treating you? I know it's been stupid hot.

2
00:00:11.946 --> 00:00:19.624
Andre Williams: Oh, it finally broke. We had like two days in the eighties, uh, which for late September is basically a gift.

3
00:00:20.654 --> 00:00:24.800
Stacy Klein: I'll take it. My commute's been fine, which is rare.

4
00:00:25.339 --> 00:00:34.205
Aisha Coleman: Okay so, before we get into uh, the numbers, is there anything that happened this quarter that I should have on my radar?

5
00:00:35.521 --> 00:00:49.661
Andre Williams: Mostly fine. We had a stretch in August where the floor was short, a couple of the newer loan officers were in training, so we ran about fifteen percent under our usual headcount on the phones.

6
00:00:49.661 --> 00:00:50.840
Andre Williams: Volume held though.

7
00:00:51.423 --> 00:00:59.205
Aisha Coleman: Yeah, I saw that. You were right around 41,000 calls for the the quarter, which is up a bit from Q2.

8
00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:02.565
Stacy Klein: That tracks. July was heavy.

9
00:01:03.108 --> 00:01:08.674
Aisha Coleman: And the PreSense screen pops, how are the loan officers feeling about those?

10
00:01:09.805 --> 00:01:20.069
Andre Williams: Good. Jimmy, one of my supervisors, says the team's gotten used to having the ad and the pages up before they even pick up.

11
00:01:20.069 --> 00:01:21.352
Andre Williams: That's been fine.

12
00:01:22.612 --> 00:01:27.384
Aisha Coleman: Great. And the QM scorecards, any coaching wins you want to talk about?

13
00:01:28.114 --> 00:01:31.063
Andre Williams: No, that's all running. Um, Nothing to raise there.

14
00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:35.471
Stacy Klein: Actually, can I jump in on something?

15
00:01:36.115 --> 00:01:36.915
Aisha Coleman: Please.

16
00:01:38.085 --> 00:01:52.801
Stacy Klein: So this is the second time this year we've hit it. When someone on my team changes a field or a picklist value on the Loan Application object, the call data stops writing over.

17
00:01:52.801 --> 00:02:01.024
Stacy Klein: Like, it just goes quiet. The The calls still come in, Five9's fine, but nothing lands on the record.

18
00:02:02.518 --> 00:02:05.478
Aisha Coleman: Okay. When did you last see that?

19
00:02:06.204 --> 00:02:17.847
Stacy Klein: Um, About three uh, weeks ago. We'd refreshed a sandbox, and the mapping between the Invoca fields and our custom objects had to be rebuilt before anything would write again.

20
00:02:17.847 --> 00:02:21.339
Stacy Klein: Took my admin the better part of a day.

21
00:02:22.673 --> 00:02:29.060
Aisha Coleman: So the refresh is what triggered it, or was it the field change before that?

22
00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:39.064
Stacy Klein: Honestly I think it's the combination. We changed a picklist value in the same window, and then the refresh... it's hard to say which one broke it first.

23
00:02:40.476 --> 00:02:53.352
Andre Williams: From my side what I notice is the gap in um, the loan officer's view. They pick up the call, they talk to the borrower, and then there's nothing on the record to follow up on.

24
00:02:54.439 --> 00:03:02.031
Aisha Coleman: That's like, the part that worries me most, yeah. I mean, how many calls are we talking about in that window?

25
00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:12.870
Stacy Klein: I'd have to go back and count. It wasn't the whole day, but it was enough that a couple of the LOs asked me about it.

26
00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:25.244
Aisha Coleman: Okay. I don't want to guess at what's driving it, so let me take it back to the team and come to you with questions rather than theories.

27
00:03:25.244 --> 00:03:30.781
Aisha Coleman: Uh, can you send me the ticket you filed, and roughly the dates?

28
00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.204
Stacy Klein: I'll forward it after this.

29
00:03:34.523 --> 00:03:40.854
Aisha Coleman: Perfect. And Andre, is there anything on the routing side I should know about, with the staffing thing?

30
00:03:41.948 --> 00:03:45.807
Andre Williams: No, that's internal. We shuffled some shifts around, that's all.

31
00:03:46.355 --> 00:03:59.392
Aisha Coleman: Okay. Let me pull up the rest. So attribution: Google Ads offline conversions are still flowing, Adobe's still seeing the the call events, that all looks normal on my end.

32
00:03:59.702 --> 00:04:00.748
Stacy Klein: Yeah, nothing there.

33
00:04:01.217 --> 00:04:09.913
Aisha Coleman: Good. Anything coming up on your side we should plan around? I know you've got that compliance review in November.

34
00:04:11.301 --> 00:04:19.218
Andre Williams: Yeah, second week. We'll probably lean on the QM recordings a little heavier than usual that month, but that's just us.

35
00:04:19.496 --> 00:04:27.597
Aisha Coleman: Makes sense. Do uh, you want to do a shorter check-in mid-October, just so we're not stacking everything on the QBR cadence?

36
00:04:28.544 --> 00:04:31.560
Andre Williams: That'd be good. Thursdays are usually clearer for me.

37
00:04:31.789 --> 00:04:35.998
Aisha Coleman: I'll send something for the fifteenth. Twenty minutes, no deck.

38
00:04:36.371 --> 00:04:38.176
Stacy Klein: So, Works for uh, me.

39
00:04:39.593 --> 00:04:45.862
Aisha Coleman: And I'll follow up on the Salesforce piece separately, once I've got your ticket and dates.

40
00:04:47.165 --> 00:04:48.050
Andre Williams: Appreciate it.

41
00:04:49.515 --> 00:04:52.803
Aisha Coleman: Alright. Anything else before I let you go?

42
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.852
Andre Williams: Nope. Thanks, Aisha.

43
00:04:55.574 --> 00:04:57.469
Stacy Klein: Thanks. Stay cool out there.

44
00:04:58.384 --> 00:04:59.725
Aisha Coleman: You too. Talk soon.
